On Feb 4, 2:11 pm, JMonkey <jfrankmil...@gmail.com> wrote:
> On Feb 3, 6:23 pm, YARGRNP <YARG...@mailinator.temp.com> wrote:

> > My question is: how does one maximize/optimize the desirable
> > permanent  intrinsics one could potentially acquire?  I
> > suppose this depends on  race/role and on the edibility of
> > rings, etc, so what is the best  character to start with,
> > and should I scum until I get a particular ring,  or
> > anything?

> The first intrinsic you should work to achieve is poison
> resistance, which is gotten by eating corpses. Quivering blobs
> and shriekers are a favorite, as they are not themselves
> poisonous. You're not guaranteed to get it from any one
> corpse, but if you eat enough of them, you eventually will.
> Corpses that are poisonous can also give you poison resistance
> (soldier ants, homunculi, killer bees, etc.) but you'll need
> extrinsic poison resistance through a ring or amulet of poison
> resistances or a ring of sustain ability. Sustain ability
> won't protect you from the hit point damage that a poison
> corpse will bring, but it will protect you from losing
> strength. Eating a poisonous corpse does not have the
> possibility of instadeath as it does from poisoned weapons or
> poisoned spikes.

The hit points are usually manageable, except in the very
earliest parts of the game.  The loss of strength isn't an issue
if you have a means of restoring it.  Once you've got a Unicorn
horn, you should be eating all poisonous corpses, unless your
hit points are particularly low.  Even before then, if you have
enough strength, it might be worthwhile.

> Next, you need reflection, which can be gotten from an amulet
> of reflection (50% chance in Sokoban) or silver dragon scale
> mail, which is typically gotten through a wish, though you can
> also make it by wearing silver dragon scales and reading a
> scroll of enchant armor.  Magic resistance is also a high
> priority, though probably second to reflection. Artifacts such
> as Magicbane or the Eyes of the Overworld grant it, but
> typically one wears a cloak of magic resistance or grey dragon
> scale mail. GDSM or SDSM are typical early wishes from a magic
> lamp.

I'd put magic resistance before reflection, since it also
protects against polymorph traps.

> Becoming very fast should come next, and the only way to do so
> permanently is with speed boots. Potions of speed will do so
> for a limited time.

Very fast is good, but just fast is already something, and some
characters have that automatically beyond a certain point.

> Telepathy can only be gained from an amulet or helm of ESP or, more
> easily, by eating a floating eye, and the chance is 100%. The floating
> eye version only works  when you're blinded, so you'll need a
> blindfold or a towel to exercise this power. Eat a floating eye at
> your first opportunity. Eating an amulet of ESP gives you the same
> type of telepathy as eating a floating eye, and with floating eyes so
> common, it's not worth it.

The ESP from an amulet isn't exactly like that from a floating
eye.  As you say, it doesn't require blinding.  But it doesn't
have the same range, either.  I find ESP very useful, if it
doesn't take a slot needed for something else.

> The other intrinsics you'll need -- fire, cold, shock, sleep
> -- can be gotten from corpses. Sleep can be gotten from eating
> elves. If the monster uses fire or cold, eating it will give
> you the resistance, generally speaking. Gelatinous cubes, some
> molds and some puddings also confer resistances.
> Disintegration resistance can only be gained from a black
> dragon corpse, but reflection handles that nicely, so it's not
> absolutely necessary. You'll get these once you're crowed as
> well. Shock is difficult, so I often don't get that until
> crowning.  You also wear the appropriate rings to fill gaps
> until then.

Shock resistance isn't that important.  But I've gotten it more
than once by eating corpses.  (I forget which ones, but you get
a message "you feel grounded".)

> You'll also need levitation to pass over water, but not until
> you reach Medusa's isle, which usually about halfway down.

There are other ways of handling the Medusa level.  My favorite
is still using scrolls of earth to produce bolders, then
creating a path with them.

> A ring of levitation is easiest, because you won't have to
> remove your speed boots,  but water walking boots or boots of
> levitation also work. Make sure they're not cursed.

By the time you get to Medusa's level, you should be able to
uncurse things.

